    title: "TFSA vs. RRSP: Which Should You Prioritize First?",
    slug: "tfsa-vs-rrsp",
    image: "/resources/tfsa-vs-rrsp.jpg",
    date: "2026-08-13",
    summary: "There's no universal right answer — but there is a right question to ask yourself, and it comes down to more than just your current paycheque.",
    body: [
      "This is the most common Canadian investing question, and most answers online oversimplify it into \"TFSA if you're young, RRSP if you're older.\" The real deciding factor isn't your age — it's your current marginal tax rate versus your expected tax rate when you eventually withdraw the money, plus a few government-benefit side effects that most people never factor in.",
      "## The core tradeoff",
      "RRSP contributions are deducted from this year's taxable income, so they're most valuable when your tax rate is high right now. TFSA contributions get no upfront deduction, but every dollar of growth and every withdrawal is completely tax-free, forever — which matters most if you expect to be in a similar or higher tax bracket later, or if you value never owing tax on the money again.",
      "## What your marginal rate actually looks like in 2026",
      "The federal brackets for 2026 are 14% up to $58,523, 20.5% from $58,524 to $117,045, 26% from $117,046 to $181,440, 29% from $181,441 to $258,482, and 33% above that. Provincial tax stacks on top of these federal rates and varies by province, so your true combined marginal rate is higher than the federal number alone — often somewhere in the 30-40% range for middle-income earners, higher still at the top. A $5,000 RRSP contribution made while sitting in the 20.5% federal bracket saves you roughly $1,000 in federal tax alone the year you contribute, before provincial savings are even counted.",
      "## Why your retirement tax rate matters more than people think",
      "The deduction you get today is only half the equation. Every dollar you eventually withdraw from an RRSP (or RRIF, which it converts to) gets taxed as regular income in the year you take it out. If your income in retirement ends up lower than it is now, you effectively deducted at a high rate and will pay tax at a lower one — a genuine win. But high earners who contribute heavily for decades sometimes build RRIF balances large enough that mandatory minimum withdrawals after age 71 push their retirement income back into a similar bracket to where they started, shrinking the advantage.",
      "## The clawback trap most people don't see coming",
      "This is the part most comparisons skip entirely. Old Age Security has a recovery tax — commonly called the OAS clawback — that starts reducing your OAS payments once your net income crosses a threshold: $95,323 for the 2026 income year. Above that line, you lose 15 cents of OAS for every extra dollar of net income, until OAS is fully clawed back at $152,062 (ages 65-74). RRSP and RRIF withdrawals count as net income and can push a retiree over that threshold. Lower-income seniors relying on the Guaranteed Income Supplement face a similar problem — GIS is income-tested, and RRIF withdrawals reduce it. TFSA withdrawals count as neither. They never touch your net income, never trigger the OAS clawback, and never reduce GIS — which is a real advantage for anyone who expects to rely on government retirement benefits.",
      "## If your employer offers RRSP matching, that usually comes first",
      "An employer RRSP match is an immediate, guaranteed return that has nothing to do with your tax bracket — turning down free matching dollars to prioritize a TFSA instead almost never makes mathematical sense. Contribute at least enough to capture the full match before weighing TFSA versus additional, unmatched RRSP room.",
      "## A simple rule of thumb",
      "If you're early in your career and in a lower tax bracket, TFSA contributions often make more sense — you're not sacrificing much of a deduction now, and decades of tax-free growth compounds significantly, with none of the future clawback exposure. If you're in your peak earning years and in a high bracket, the RRSP deduction becomes more valuable, especially if you expect a meaningfully lower income in retirement.",
      "## You don't actually have to choose",
      "Contribution room in one account doesn't reduce room in the other — they're entirely independent. Many Canadians end up using both simultaneously: RRSP for the deduction and employer matching during high-income years, TFSA for flexible, tax-free growth that stays invisible to both the CRA and any income-tested benefit calculation.",
      "If you're running both accounts, our TFSA + RRSP combo tracker keeps contribution room, deduction limits, and growth for both in one dashboard — so you can see the full picture instead of switching between two spreadsheets.",
    ],
